$DIR/main.p:25:9: cannot return p, which points into the closure mk() returns, built in h's frame
    p points into the closure mk() returns, built in h's frame. mk() returns a
    closure, which it builds, with the variables it uses, in a region of its
    caller's frame, h's (at main.p:24); they live as long as that frame (section
    11.5). That frame is reclaimed when h returns, so p would point at memory
    that no longer holds the value.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the value itself, which copies it, or allocate it so that it outlives
    the function.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:32:9: cannot return q, which points into the closure mk() returns, built in h2's frame
    q points into the closure mk() returns, built in h2's frame. mk() returns a
