    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:39:11: cannot keep p past this iteration of the loop: it points into the region of mk(), and it is stored in keep, declared outside the loop (main.p:37)
    The pointer p points to a variable that the closure mk() returns uses. A
    function that returns a closure builds it, with the variables it uses, in a
    region its caller provides (spec section 11.5). The region of a call in a
    loop is part of this frame, and the call refills the same region on every
    iteration, so the variable p points to on one iteration is overwritten on
    the next. Keeping p past the iteration, in keep, declared outside the loop
    (main.p:37), would leave it seeing the next iteration's variable, not this
    one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    Use p before the iteration ends, or keep a copy of the value it points to
    instead of the pointer.
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)

