$DIR/main.p:83:2: cannot store p in package variable saved, which outlives Save's frame
    p points into the closure mk() returns, built in Save's frame. mk() returns
    a closure, which it builds, with the variables it uses, in a region of its
    caller's frame, Save's (at main.p:82); they live as long as that frame
    (section 11.5). When Save returns, its frame is reclaimed, but package
    variables live for the whole program, so package variable saved would be
    left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of Save's frame, or allocate it so
    that it lives as long as package variable saved.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:90:2: cannot store &x in *out, which outlives x
    &x is a pointer to x. x is a local variable of into (declared at main.p:89).
    When into returns, x's memory is reclaimed, but *out is reached through out,
    which points to memory that into's caller owns and that lives longer. It
    would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value instead of a pointer to it, changing the type of *out to
    match. This copies x, or moves it if it is an owner:
        *out = x
    Or allocate x and store its owner, an owned[int], which moves into *out:
        *out = new(int)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
